{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails to address the 'acting on' component of the prompt. While framing theory choice as a methodological rule for future testing avoids induction, Popper explicitly admits scientists choose theories to *act on* (e.g., build bridges). Action inherently relies on an expectation of future reliability (pragmatic success). By reducing the justification solely to 'potential for future learning/falsification,' the target ignores that acting on a theory requires assuming it will not fail catastrophically before it can be falsified. This assumption functions as a pragmatic wager on future performance based on past results, which smuggles in the very inductive principle (that the future will resemble the past) that Popper rejected.","premise":"Action implies an expectation of future reliability that mere 'readiness for falsification' does not provide.","premises_essential":["58f447adb4faeb1b531f904bcc782a3c0b8815c57d18f4c84b29a8b4cc9164e3"],"successor_question":"If we cannot assume a well-corroborated theory is more likely to work in practice than a refuted one, on what non-inductive basis does Popper justify building a bridge using the former rather than the latter?","target":"58f447adb4faeb1b531f904bcc782a3c0b8815c57d18f4c84b29a8b4cc9164e3"}]}